  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ since they are the only values that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and the endpoints $x=0,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c5368ebe232e9ad044cacb16300fb2f3f1747fefd326f4be4e0c22d0e3a7069d.png)",
